BC(1)                          P32W Unix                           BC(1)

NAME
       bc - an arbitrary-precision calculator language

SYNOPSIS
       bc [-l] [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
       bc reads the files in turn, then the standard input, running
       each statement as soon as it has been read. Numbers are
       decimal and may have any number of digits before and after
       the point; arithmetic is exact except where scale cuts off
       (truncates, never rounds) the digits of a result.

       An expression on its own is evaluated and its value printed
       (and kept as last); an assignment is not printed. Statements
       are separated by newlines or semicolons; a backslash at the
       end of a line joins it to the next. Comments are /* ... */
       and # to the end of the line.

       Numbers: digits 0-9 and A-F, with an optional point, read in
       base ibase (a single digit A-F is always 10-15; a digit too
       big for the base counts as ibase - 1).

       Names: variables (a, total), arrays (a[i], index 0 to
       16777215, unset elements 0) and functions (a()) have separate
       names; all start with a lower-case letter and may go on with
       letters, digits and _. A variable never set is 0.

       Special variables:

              scale  digits kept after the point by * / % ^ sqrt
                     (0 to 32767; 0 at the start, 20 with -l)
              ibase  the base of numbers read (2 to 16, 10)
              obase  the base of numbers printed (2 to 999, 10;
                     above 16 each digit is printed as a blank and
                     a decimal number)
              last   the last number printed

       Operators, the tightest-binding first:

              ++ --         before or after a variable
              - (unary)     -2^2 is 4
              ^             a power: the exponent is an integer;
                            right to left
              * / %
              + -
              = += -= *= /= %= ^=   right to left
              < <= > >= == !=       1 or 0
              !
              &&
              ||            (both evaluate only what they need)

       An assignment binds more tightly than a comparison, so
       x = 1 < 2 sets x to 1. The scale of a result is the larger
       scale of the operands for + and -; for a*b the smaller of
       their scales added together and the larger of scale and
       their own scales; scale for a/b; for a%b, a - (a/b)*b worked
       out exactly; for a^n (n > 0) the smaller of n times a's
       scale and the larger of scale and a's; 1/a^-n at scale for
       n < 0.

       Functions: sqrt(x) (at the larger of scale and x's scale),
       length(x) (the number of significant digits), scale(x) (the
       digits after its point).

       Statements:

              expression           print it (unless an assignment)
              "string"             print the string as it is
              print list           print expressions and strings
                                   (no newline added; in these
                                   strings \n \t \\ \q (a quote)
                                   \a \b \f \r are escapes)
              { statements }
              if (e) s [else s]
              while (e) s
              for (e1; e2; e3) s   any of the three may be empty
              break  continue
              return  return (e)   in a function (0 by default)
              quit                 stop, as soon as it is read
              halt                 stop, when it is run

       A function is defined by

              define name(p, q, a[]) {
                  auto r, s, t[]
                  statements
              }

       The parameters and autos are local (dynamically scoped: a
       function called from it sees them); arrays are passed by
       value as name[]. A new definition replaces the old one.

OPTIONS
       -l     load the math library and set scale to 20. Its
              functions work at scale + 10 digits and cut the result
              to scale:

              s(x)   sine of x (radians)
              c(x)   cosine of x
              a(x)   arctangent of x
              l(x)   natural logarithm of x (x > 0)
              e(x)   e to the power x
              j(n,x) Bessel function of integer order n

EXAMPLES
       echo "2^100" | bc
       echo "scale=50; 4*a(1)" | bc -l       pi to 50 digits
       echo "obase=16; 48879" | bc
       bc -l prog.b

       define f(n) {
           if (n <= 1) return (1)
           return (n * f(n - 1))
       }
       f(50)

ENVIRONMENT
       BC_LINE_LENGTH  a number too long for a line is split by a
                       backslash and newline before this column
                       less 1 (70, so 68 digits and the backslash
                       to a line; 0 never splits)

DIAGNOSTICS
       Syntax errors name the file (or the line of the standard
       input) and skip the rest of the line. Errors when running
       (divide by zero, square root of a negative number, an
       undefined function, a bad ibase, obase or scale, an array
       index out of range, calls nested more than 2000 deep, out of
       memory) are reported and the statement is abandoned; bc goes
       on with the next one. A non-integer exponent is a warning
       (its fraction is dropped).

EXIT STATUS
       0; 1 if a file could not be opened; 2 for a usage error.

SEE ALSO
       expr(1), basic(1)

BUGS
       The digits are kept one to a byte, and P32W is slow: the -l
       functions at scale 20 take a second or two each.

       There is no read(), no void functions, no reference (*)
       array parameters, no limits or warranty.
